{"cases":[{"attack":true,"case":"The target's distinction between 'procedural necessity' and 'epistemic inference' collapses when addressing the prompt's specific challenge regarding 'choosing which theory to act on.' Popper explicitly allows for acting on the best-corroborated theory because it is the most rational choice given current knowledge, not merely a move in a dialectical game. By reducing the preference to a rule that offers 'no guidance on what to believe,' the target fails to defend the *defensibility* of the preference on Popper's own terms; instead, it accepts the critic's charge that the preference is arbitrary or non-epistemic, thereby failing to show how the preference avoids smuggling in induction while still being a rational basis for action.","premise":"Popper argues that preferring the better-corroborated theory is rational because it has withstood severe tests, implying an epistemic advantage in terms of verisimilitude (closeness to truth), not just procedural status.","successor_question":"If corroboration provides no epistemic reason to believe a theory is closer to the truth, on what grounds does Popper claim it is 'rational' to prefer it for practical action rather than flipping a coin?","target":"fcc20d26771443f9f9e8acd6740a395ed0cbc3e6f7b8704366af89a6eb510fee"}]}